---
/**
 * 404.astro — the not-found page.
 *
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
 *
 * Before it, a 404 on the live site served ASTRO'S BUILT-IN error page.
 * Measured, not assumed:
 *
 *     $ curl -sI https://www.samueldwoo.com/nope-does-not-exist
 *     HTTP/2 404 ... server: Vercel ... x-vercel-cache: MISS
 *     $ curl -s  https://www.samueldwoo.com/nope-does-not-exist | head
 *     <title>404: Not Found</title>
 *     html { background: var(--black); color-scheme: dark; }
 *     --black: #13151A;  --accent-light: #E0CCFA;
 *
 * A dark, purple-accented, monospace page with no nav, no footer and no way
 * back, on a site that is otherwise light sage. It was also served by the
 * SERVERLESS FUNCTION: .vercel/output/config.json ended with
 * `{"src":"^/.*$","dest":"_render","status":404}`, so every mistyped URL and
 * every stale inbound link paid a function invocation (and a cold start) to be
 * told it was wrong.
 *
 * HOW ADDING THIS FILE CHANGES THE DEPLOY — read this before touching it.
 *
 * @astrojs/vercel picks the /404 route out of the manifest and branches on
 * whether it is prerendered (node_modules/@astrojs/vercel/dist/index.js:354-367):
 *
 *     dest: fourOhFourRoute.isPrerendered ? "/404.html" : ... NODE_PATH
 *
 * This page prerenders (the project is `output: 'static'` and this file does not
 * opt out), so the catch-all becomes `dest: "/404.html"` and the page is served
 * as a STATIC FILE at the CDN edge with a correct 404 status — no function, no
 * cold start. Astro emits it as `404.html` and not `404/index.html` even under
 * `build.format: 'directory'`, because /404 is in Astro's STATUS_CODE_PAGES set
 * (node_modules/astro/dist/core/build/common.js:4).
 *
 * If you ever add `export const prerender = false` here, that reverts to the
 * function path. Don't, unless a 404 genuinely needs request data.
 *
 * NO `.reveal` CLASSES ON THIS PAGE, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE.
 *
 * `html.js-anim .reveal:not(.is-visible)` pins content to opacity 0 until
 * script.js reveals it. script.js does query `.reveal` globally rather than
 * inside `.band`, so it *would* pick these up — but an error page is the single
 * worst place on the site to make content visible only if a 467KB script chain
 * loaded and ran. Somebody landing here has already had one thing fail. So every
 * element is visible from first paint and the page is legible with JS off,
 * scripts blocked, or the CDN having a bad day.
 *
 * WHAT IT REUSES, AND WHY NOTHING NEW WAS ADDED
 *
 * Every class here already exists in public/styles.css and is already exercised
 * by another page, so this page cannot be the reason a stylesheet changes:
 *   .content .band .band-inner   layout scaffolding (styles.css:411-425)
 *   .kicker .section-title
 *   .section-intro               the type ramp (styles.css:602-638)
 *   .resume-line .btn-resume     the site's ONE primary sage pill
 *                                (styles.css:1254-1270). The class name says
 *                                "resume" because the résumé button is where it
 *                                was first needed; it is a generic filled pill
 *                                and reusing it beats inventing a second one in
 *                                a stylesheet this page is not allowed to touch.
 *   .foot-link                   sage inline link, 44px tap target
 *                                (styles.css:1417-1424). Required, not
 *                                decorative: `a { color: inherit;
 *                                text-decoration: none }` (styles.css:139) means
 *                                an unclassed <a> is indistinguishable from
 *                                body text.
 * No new stylesheet and no new script is referenced. <Base> supplies exactly
 * the same five sheets and eight scripts as every other page.
 *
 * `page="error"` + `noindex` are Base.astro's error-page path: cross-page nav
 * hrefs, no active nav link, `robots: noindex, follow`, and no canonical.
 * `follow` rather than `nofollow` on purpose — the links out of here are the
 * three real pages and a crawler should absolutely take them.
 */
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import SiteFoot from '../components/SiteFoot.astro';
---

<Base
    title="Page not found — Samuel Woo"
    description="That page isn't here. Links back to the rest of Samuel Woo's site."
    page="error"
    skipTarget="notfound"
    noindex
>
    <main class="content">
        <section id="notfound" class="band" aria-labelledby="notfound-title">
            <div class="band-inner">
                <p class="kicker">Error 404</p>
                <h1 id="notfound-title" class="section-title">This page isn't here</h1>
                <p class="section-intro">
                    Either the address has a typo in it or I moved something and
                    didn't leave a forwarding note. Both are fixable from here.
                </p>

                {/* The primary way out. Root-absolute for the same reason every
                     path in Base.astro is: URLs are directory-format, so a `./`
                     or bare relative href resolves one level deep on any page
                     but `/`. */}
                <p class="resume-line">
                    <a href="/" class="btn-resume">Back to the home page <span aria-hidden="true">&#8599;</span></a>
                </p>

                {/* Not a dead end: every other destination the site has, plus a
                     way to tell me the link is broken. These are the same three
                     pages the nav and the command palette offer, so there is no
                     second list to keep correct. */}
                <p style="margin-top: 28px;">
                    <a href="/projects" class="foot-link">Projects</a>
                    <span aria-hidden="true" style="color: var(--line2); padding: 0 10px;">&middot;</span>
                    <a href="/travel" class="foot-link">Travel</a>
                    <span aria-hidden="true" style="color: var(--line2); padding: 0 10px;">&middot;</span>
                    <a href="mailto:samuel.dw.woo@gmail.com?subject=Broken%20link%20on%20samueldwoo.com" class="foot-link">Report a broken link</a>
                </p>

                <SiteFoot right="built" />
            </div>
        </section>
    </main>
</Base>
